t fragment)**\n\nfragment_retries=1, first fragment always raises HTTPError. count goes 0 -> try fails -> count=1, count(1) <= 1 so report_retry. Loop: count(1) <= 1: try fails -> count=2, count(2) <= 1 false, no report. Exit loop. count(2) > 1: fatal (i==0) -> report_error, return False.\n\n```python\ndef test_http_error_exhausted_fatal(monkeypatch):\n    fd = make_fd({'fragment_retries': 1})\n    info_dict = {'fragments': [{'url': 'f1'}]}\n    errors = []\n    def fake_download(ctx, url, info_dict_):\n        raise compat_urllib_error.HTTPError(url, 404, 'not found', None, None)\n    def fake_error(msg):\n        errors.append(msg)\n    ...\n    assert result is False\n    assert errors == ['giving up after 1 fragment retries']\n```\n\n**Test 7: HTTPError exhausted, non-fatal (skip)**\n\nskip_unavailable_fragments=True (default), fragment_retries=0, second fragment raises HTTPError. count=0 -> try fails -> count=1, count(1) <= 0 false. Exit. count(1) > 0: not fatal -> report_skip_fragment, continue. Then finish, return True.\n\n```python\ndef test_http_error_exhausted_nonfatal_skips_fragment(monkeypatch):\n    fd = make_fd({'fragment_retries': 0})\n    info_dict = {'fragments': [{'url': 'f1'}, {'url': 'f2'}]}\n    def fake_download(ctx, url, info_dict_):\n        if url == 'f2':\n            raise compat_urllib_error.HTTPError(url, 404, 'not found', None, None)\n        return True, b'data'\n    skipped = []\n    def fake_skip(frag_index):\n        skipped.append(frag_index)\n    ...\n    assert result is True\n    assert skipped == [2]\n    assert calls['append'] == [b'data']  # only f1 appended\n```\n\n**Test 8: DownloadError non-fatal skip**\n\nskip_unavailable_fragments=True, second fragment raises DownloadError. fatal = i==0 or not skip -> i=1, skip=True -> fatal=False. report_skip_fragment, break. Continue. Return True.\n\n```python\ndef test_download_error_nonfatal_skips_fragment(monkeypatch):\n    fd = make_fd()\n    info_dict = {'fragments': [{'url': 'f1'}, {'url': 'f2'}]}\n    def fake_download(ctx, url, info_dict_):\n        if url == 'f2':\n            raise DownloadError('boom')\n        return True, b'data'\n    ...\n    assert result is True\n    assert skipped == [2]\n```\n\n**Test 9: DownloadError fatal raises**\n\nskip_unavailable_fragments=False -> fatal=True for all. DownloadError raised -> re-raised.\n\n```python\ndef test_download_error_fatal_reraises(monkeypatch):\n    fd = make_fd({'skip_unavailable_fragments': False})\n    info_dict = {'fragments': [{'url': 'f1'}]}\n    def fake_download(ctx, url, info_dict_):\n        raise DownloadError('boom')\n    with pytest.raises(DownloadError):\n        fd.real_download('dest', info_dict)\n```\n\n**Test 10: _download_fragment returns False**\n\n```python\ndef test_download_fragment_failure_returns_false(monkeypatch):\n    fd = make_fd()\n    info_dict = {'fragments': [{'url': 'f1'}]}\n    def fake_download(ctx, url, info_dict_):\n        return False, None\n    ...\n    assert result is False\n    assert not calls['append']\n    assert not calls['finish']\n```\n\n**Test 11: missing fragment_base_url with path-only fragment raises AssertionError**\n\n```python\ndef test_missing_base_url_asserts(monkeypatch):\n    fd = make_fd()\n    info_dict = {'fragments': [{'path': 'seg.m4s'}]}\n    with pytest.raises(AssertionError):\n        fd.real_download('dest', info_dict)\n```\n\nHmm, this covers the assert failing branch. But wait \u2014 AssertionError isn't caught by the except clauses, so it propagates. But then _finish_frag_download is never called. That's fine for the test.\n\nActually, should I include this? It covers the branch where the assert fails. For line coverage it's not needed, but for branch coverage of the assert it is. I'll include it.\n\nAlso, `skip_unavailable_fragments=False` with HTTPError exhausted: fatal=True via `not skip_unavailable_fragments` even for i>0. That covers the other side of line 40's `or`. Let me add:\n\n**Test 12: HTTPError exhausted with skip_unavailable_fragments=False on non-first fragment**\n\n```python\ndef test_http_error_exhausted_no_skip(monkeypatch):\n    fd = make_fd({'fragment_retries': 0, 'skip_unavailable_fragments': False})\n    info_dict = {'fragments': [{'url': 'f1'}, {'url': 'f2'}]}\n    # f1 succeeds, f2 raises HTTPError -> fatal because skip_unavailable_fragments is False\n    assert result is False\n    assert errors == ['giving up after 0 fragment retries']\n```\n\nThis covers `fatal = i == 0 or not skip_unavailable_fragments` where the second operand matters, and line 75 (report_error + return False) for a non-first fragment.\n\nAlso, DownloadError with fatal on non-first fragment (skip=False) -> raise. That's covered by test 9 essentially (first fragment). The `if not fatal` branch false -> raise. Test 9 covers line 69 raise. Good.\n\nNow, what about `report_retry_fragment` when count <= fragment_retries \u2014 covered in test 5. The branch where count > fragment_retries (no report) \u2014 covered in test 6/7 (the last retry doesn't report). Good.\n\nNow let me think about the ctx dict. The real code:\n\n```python\n        ctx = {\n            'filename': filename,\n            'total_frags': len(fragments),\n        }\n        self._prepare_and_start_frag_download(ctx)\n```\n\nThen `ctx['fragment_index']` is accessed at line 36. In the real FragmentFD, `_prepare_frag_download` sets `ctx['fragment_index']`? Let me check the actual source of youtube-dl fragment.py:\n\n```python\n    def _prepare_and_start_frag_download(self, ctx):\n        self._prepare_frag_download(ctx)\n        self._start_frag_download(ctx)\n\n    def _prepare_frag_download(self, ctx):\n        if 'live' not in ctx:\n            ctx['live'] = False\n        if not ctx['live']:\n            total_frags = ctx['total_frags']\n            # not (we're downloading only the fragments included in\n            # info_dict['fragments']) or...\n            ad_frags = ctx.get('ad_frags', 0)\n            if ad_frags and not ctx.get('skip_ad_breaks'):\n                self._hook_progress({\n                    'status': 'finished',\n                    'total_frags': total_frags,\n                    'fragment_count': total_frags - ad_frags,\n                })\n            self.to_screen('[%s] Total fragments: %d' %\n                           (self.FD_NAME, total_frags - ad_frags))\n        self.report_destination(ctx['filename'])\n        dl = YoutubeDL(self._ydl.params...)\n```\n\nHmm, I don't remember exactly. Let me check `_start_frag_download`:\n\n```python\n    def _start_frag_download(self, ctx):\n        total_frags = ctx['total_frags']\n        # This dict stores the download progress, it is updated by the progress\n        # hook and we can use it to calculate the ETA.\n        ctx['fragment_index'] = 0\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, actually I think in newer versions:\n\n```python\n        ctx.update({\n            'fragment_index': 0,\n            ...\n        })\n```\n\nWait, actually in youtube-dl's fragment.py, `_start_frag_download` has:\n\n```python\n    def _start_frag_download(self, ctx):\n        total_frags = ctx['total_frags']\n        # This dict stores the download progress, it is updated by the progress\n        # hook and we can use it to calculate the ETA.\n        ctx['fragment_index'] = 0\n        ctx['fragment_count'] = ...\n```\n\nHmm, I'm not 100% sure. But since we're mocking `_prepare_and_start_frag_download`, we control it. Our mock sets `ctx['fragment_index'] = 0` by default (or a custom value for resume tests). That's realistic.\n\nActually wait \u2014 should I mock these methods, or use the real ones? Using real ones would require a lot of infrastructure (YoutubeDL instance, real file I/O, etc.). Mocking is the standard unit-test approach here and gives precise control. The youtube-dl project itself tests DashSegmentsFD via integration tests with a local HTTP server, but for pytest unit tests, mocking is appropriate.\n\nHowever, there's a subtlety: the task says \"Create new pytest test functions that execute all missing lines and branches\". Mocking the helper methods is fine as long as real_download's lines 19-80 all execute.\n\nLet me now check the actual signatures with get_info to make sure I get constructor args right and method names right.\n\nLet me call get_info for:\n- DashSegmentsFD\n- FragmentFD._prepare_and_start_frag_download\n- FragmentFD._download_fragment\n- FragmentFD._append_fragment\n- FragmentFD._finish_frag_download\n- FragmentFD.report_retry_fragment\n- FragmentFD.report_skip_fragment\n- FileDownloader.report_error\n- FileDownloader.__init__\n\nI'll batch these calls.\n\nActually, one concern: `monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_download_fragment', fake)` \u2014 monkeypatch.setattr with a string name on an object works: `monkeypatch.setattr(obj, \"name\", value)`. Yes.\n\nBut there's a catch: if the method is accessed via `self._download_fragment`, setting an instance attribute shadows it. Good.\n\nAlternatively, I could create a subclass overriding methods. That's cleaner and avoids monkeypatch issues:\n\n```python\nclass FakeDashSegmentsFD(DashSegmentsFD):\n    def __init__(self, ydl, params, behavior):\n        ...\n```\n\nBut monkeypatch on instance is fine and simple. Actually, let me use a helper that patches instance methods via monkeypatch \u2014 monkeypatch auto-undoes, avoiding state pollution. \n\nHmm, but one issue: monkeypatch.setattr(fd, 'name', value) requires that the attribute exists (it does, on the class). It will set an instance attribute and on undo, delete it. Good.\n\nNow, the FakeYDL: FileDownloader.__init__ does:\n\n```python\nclass FileDownloader(object):\n    def __init__(self, ydl, params):\n        self._ydl = ydl\n        self.ydl = ydl\n        self.params = params\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, let me check. Actually:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(self, ydl, params):\n        \"\"\"Create a FileDownloader object.\"\"\"\n        self.ydl = ydl\n        self.params = params\n        self._progress_hooks = []\n        ...\n```\n\nAnd `report_error`:\n\n```python\n    def report_error(self, *args, **kargs):\n        self.ydl.report_error(*args, **kargs)\n```\n\nHmm, actually FileDownloader.report_error might be:\n\n```python\n    def report_error(self, *args, **kargs):\n        self.ydl.report_error(*args, **kargs)\n```\n\nHmm wait, in FileDownloader there's `to_screen`:\n\n```python\n    def to_screen(self, *args, **kargs):\n        self.ydl.to_screen(*args, **kargs)\n```\n\nAnd `report_error` in FileDownloader... Let me check. In youtube_dl/downloader/common.py:\n\n```python\nclass FileDownloader(object):\n    ...\n    def to_screen(self, *args, **kargs):\n        self.ydl.to_screen(*args, **kargs)\n\n    def report_error(self, *args, **kargs):\n        self.ydl.report_error(*args, **kargs)\n```\n\nHmm, actually I think `report_error` might not exist on FileDownloader; it might be `self.report_error` defined in common.py as:\n\n```python\n    def report_error(self, *args, **kargs):\n        self.ydl.report_error(*args, **kargs)\n```\n\nHmm, I'm not sure. Let me check with get_info. Actually in youtube-dl, `FileDownloader` has:\n\n```python\n    def _hook_progress(self, status):\n        for ph in self._progress_hooks:\n            ph(status)\n```\n\nand\n\n```python\n    def report_error(self, *args, **kargs):\n        self.ydl.report_error(*args, **kargs)\n```\n\nHmm, I don't think FileDownloader defines report_error... Actually wait, in downloader/common.py:\n\n```python\nclass FileDownloader(object):\n    ...\n    def to_screen(self, *args, **kargs):\n        \"\"\"Print message to stdout if not in quiet mode\"\"\"\n        return self.ydl.to_screen(*args, **kargs)\n\n    def to_stderr(self, message):\n        \"\"\"Print message to stderr\"\"\"\n        print(message, file=sys.stderr)\n\n    def report_warning(self, message):\n        ...\n\n    def report_error(self, *args, **kargs):\n        self.ydl.report_error(*args, **kargs)\n```\n\nHmm. Actually I recall `report_error` is defined in common.py. Let me just check with get_info.\n\nAlso `report_retry_fragment` in fragment.py:\n\n```python\n    def report_retry_fragment(self, err, frag_index, count, retries):\n        self.to_screen('[%s] Got error: %s' % (self.FD_NAME, str(err)))\n        self.to_screen('[%s] Retrying fragment %d (attempt %d/%d)...'\n                       % (self.FD_NAME, frag_index, count, retries))\n\n    def report_skip_fragment(self, frag_index):\n        self.to_screen('[%s] Skipping fragment %d...' % (self.FD_NAME, frag_index))\n```\n\nOK. Since I'm mocking report_retry_fragment, report_skip_fragment, report_error on the instance, the FakeYDL doesn't need those. But `to_screen` might be called by... no, since all reporting methods are mocked. But `_prepare_and_start_frag_download` is mocked too. So FakeYDL just needs `params`. But to be safe, give it to_screen and report_warning no-ops.\n\nWait, actually \u2014 do I even need to mock report_error? In test 6, report_error is called. If not mocked, it calls self.ydl.report_error which would raise... YoutubeDL.report_error raises DownloadError after printing. With FakeYDL lacking report_error, AttributeError. So yes, mock report_error on the fd instance.\n\nLet me now also double check `urljoin` from youtube_dl.utils:\n\n```python\ntry:\n    from urllib.parse import urljoin as compat_urljoin\nexcept ImportError:\n    ...\nurljoin = compat_urljoin\n```\n\nHmm, actually in youtube_dl/utils.py:\n\n```python\nfrom .compat import compat_str, compat_urllib_parse_urlparse, ...\n...\ndef urljoin(base, path):\n    ...\n```\n\nActually youtube-dl defines its own `urljoin`:\n\n```python\ndef urljoin(base, path):\n    if isinstance(path, bytes):\n        path = path.decode('utf-8')\n    if not isinstance(path, compat_str) or not path:\n        return None\n    if not isinstance(base, compat_str) or not base:\n        return None\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, there is a custom urljoin in youtube_dl/utils.py. Let me check with get_info('urljoin'). Either way, 'http://example.com/base/' + 'seg1.m4s' should join properly.\n\nNow, about HTTPError construction: `compat_urllib_error.HTTPError(url, code, msg, hdrs, fp)`. In py3, HTTPError(url, code, msg, hdrs, fp). fp=None is allowed (it becomes an addinfourl-ish?). Actually HTTPError.__init__ calls urllib.response... In py3:\n\n```python\nclass HTTPError(URLError, urllib.response.addinfourl):\n    def __init__(self, url, code, msg, hdrs, fp):\n        self.code = code\n        self.msg = msg\n        self.hdrs = hdrs\n        self.fp = fp\n        self.filename = url\n        if fp is not None:\n            self._set_fp(fp)\n        else:\n            self.fp = None\n        URLError.__init__(self, msg)\n```\n\nHmm, in newer py3 versions there's a `reason` property etc. Constructing with fp=None works fine. `str(err)` \u2014 HTTPError.__str__ returns str(self.reason) or msg. Fine.\n\nActually wait, in Python 3.9+, HTTPError.__init__ signature is (url, code, msg, hdrs, fp). OK.\n\nNow, one more consideration: the `while count <= fragment_retries` loop with HTTPError: after the exception, `count += 1`, then `if count <= fragment_retries: report`. Then loop condition re-checked. In test 5 (retries=1): attempt 1 fails -> count=1, 1<=1 -> report. Loop: 1<=1 -> attempt 2 succeeds -> break. Good.\n\nIn test 6 (retries=1, always fails): attempt1 fail -> count=1, report. attempt2 fail -> count=2, 2<=1 false. Exit loop. count(2)>1 -> fatal -> report_error('giving up after 1 fragment retries') -> return False. Good.\n\nNow let me also think about whether `real_download` accesses `self.params.get('test', False)` \u2014 params is the dict passed. With FakeYDL, fd.params = params dict. Good.\n\nNow, about `_download_fragment` returning `(success, frag_content)`. In real FragmentFD:\n\n```python\n    def _download_fragment(self, ctx, frag_url, info_dict, headers=None, request_data=None):\n        ...\n        success = False\n        try:\n            ...\n            success = True\n        except ...\n        return success, frag_content\n```\n\nHmm, actually in the version matching this dash.py code, `_download_fragment` returns (success, frag_content). OK.\n\nLet me now write the final test file. I'll structure it with a helper to build the fd and record calls.\n\n```python\nimport pytest\n\nfrom youtube_dl.compat import compat_urllib_error\nfrom youtube_dl.downloader.dash import DashSegmentsFD\nfrom youtube_dl.utils import DownloadError\n\n\nclass FakeYDL(object):\n    def __init__(self, params=None):\n        self.params = params if params is not None else {}\n\n    def to_screen(self, *args, **kwargs):\n        pass\n\n    def report_warning(self, *args, **kwargs):\n        pass\n\n\ndef make_fd(params=None):\n    return DashSegmentsFD(FakeYDL(params))\n```\n\nWait \u2014 DashSegmentsFD.__init__? FragmentFD doesn't define __init__, FileDownloader does: `def __init__(self, ydl, params)`. So DashSegmentsFD(FakeYDL(params))? No wait: FileDownloader.__init__(self, ydl, params) \u2014 so DashSegmentsFD(ydl, params) with two args. Let me check: \n\n```python\nclass FileDownloader(object):\n    def __init__(self, ydl, params):\n```\n\nYes, two args. So `DashSegmentsFD(ydl, params)`. Let me verify with get_info.\n\nHmm, actually I recall in youtube-dl tests:\n\n```python\nfd = FDClass(params)\n```\n\nNo wait, in test_downloader_http.py:\n\n```python\nclass TestHTTP(unittest.TestCase):\n    def setUp(self):\n        ...\n        self.fd = FileDownloader(ydl, params)\n```\n\nHmm, actually:\n\n```python\ndef _build_downloader(self, params):\n    return youtube_dl.downloader.http.HttpFD(ydl, params)\n```\n\nYes, two args: (ydl, params). OK.\n\nNow the recorder helper:\n\n```python\nclass Recorder(object):\n    def __init__(self, fragment_index=0):\n        self.prepared = []\n        self.downloaded = []\n        self.appended = []\n        self.finished = []\n        self.retried = []\n        self.skipped = []\n        self.errors = []\n        self.fragment_index = fragment_index\n\n    def patch(self, fd, monkeypatch, download):\n        monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_prepare_and_start_frag_download', self._prepare)\n        monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_download_fragment', download)\n        monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_append_fragment', self._append)\n        monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_finish_frag_download', self._finish)\n        monkeypatch.setattr(fd, 'report_retry_fragment', self._retry)\n        monkeypatch.setattr(fd, 'report_skip_fragment', self._skip)\n        monkeypatch.setattr(fd, 'report_error', self._error)\n\n    def _prepare(self, ctx):\n        ctx['fragment_index'] = self.fragment_index\n        self.prepared.append(dict(ctx))\n\n    def _append(self, ctx, content):\n        self.appended.append(content)\n\n    def _finish(self, ctx):\n        self.finished.append(dict(ctx))\n\n    def _retry(self, err, frag_index, count, retries):\n        self.retried.append((frag_index, count, retries))\n\n    def _skip(self, frag_index):\n        self.skipped.append(frag_index)\n\n    def _error(self, message):\n        self.errors.append(message)\n```\n\nAnd download functions defined per-test.\n\nNow write each test.\n\nTest 1 \u2014 success, url fragments, no test mode:\n\n```python\ndef test_success_downloads_all_fragments(monkeypatch):\n    fd = make_fd()\n    rec = Recorder()\n    info_dict = {\n        'fragments': [\n            {'url': 'http://example.com/seg0'},\n            {'url': 'http://example.com/seg1'},\n        ],\n    }\n\n    def download(ctx, url, info_dict_):\n        rec.downloaded.append(url)\n        return True, b'content-' + url.encode('ascii')\n\n    rec.patch(fd, monkeypatch, download)\n\n    assert fd.real_download('video.mp4', info_dict) is True\n    assert rec.downloaded == ['http://example.com/seg0', 'http://example.com/seg1']\n    assert rec.appended == [b'content-http://example.com/seg0', b'content-http://example.com/seg1']\n    assert len(rec.finished) == 1\n    assert rec.prepared[0]['total_frags'] == 2\n    assert rec.prepared[0]['filename'] == 'video.mp4'\n```\n\nTest 2 \u2014 test mode:\n\n```python\ndef test_test_mode_downloads_only_first_fragment(monkeypatch):\n    fd = make_fd({'test': True})\n    rec = Recorder()\n    info_dict = {\n        'fragments': [\n            {'url': 'http://example.com/seg0'},\n            {'url': 'http://example.com/seg1'},\n        ],\n    }\n\n    def download(ctx, url, info_dict_):\n        rec.downloaded.append(url)\n        return True, b'content'\n\n    rec.patch(fd, monkeypatch, download)\n\n    assert fd.real_download('video.mp4', info_dict) is True\n    assert rec.downloaded == ['http://example.com/seg0']\n    assert rec.prepared[0]['total_frags'] == 1\n```\n\nTest 3 \u2014 path + base url:\n\n```python\ndef test_fragment_url_built_from_base_url_and_path(monkeypatch):\n    fd = make_fd()\n    rec = Recorder()\n    info_dict = {\n        'fragment_base_url': 'http://example.com/base/',\n        'fragments': [{'path': 'seg0.m4s'}, {'path': 'seg1.m4s'}],\n    }\n\n    def download(ctx, url, info_dict_):\n        rec.downloaded.append(url)\n        return True, b'content'\n\n    rec.patch(fd, monkeypatch, download)\n\n    assert fd.real_download('video.mp4', info_dict) is True\n    assert rec.downloaded == [\n        'http://example.com/base/seg0.m4s',\n        'http://example.com/base/seg1.m4s',\n    ]\n```\n\nTest 4 \u2014 resume:\n\n```python\ndef test_resume_skips_fragments_up_to_fragment_index(monkeypatch):\n    fd = make_fd()\n    rec = Recorder(fragment_index=2)\n    info_dict = {\n        'fragments': [\n            {'url': 'http://example.com/seg0'},\n            {'url': 'http://example.com/seg1'},\n            {'url': 'http://example.com/seg2'},\n            {'url': 'http://example.com/seg3'},\n        ],\n    }\n\n    def download(ctx, url, info_dict_):\n        rec.downloaded.append(url)\n        return T